Based on the RAA Contents PDS, here is the relevant information on Flood cover:

**Definition of Flood:**
Flood means the covering of normally dry land by water that has escaped or been released from the normal confines of:
- any lake, or any river, creek or other natural watercourse, whether or not altered or modified; or
- any reservoir, canal or dam.
[RAA Contents PDS, page 87]

**Exclusions relating to Flood:**
- Flood excludes water from Actions of the Sea, except when caused by a Storm Surge occurring at the same time your Insured Property is damaged by a Storm, or by a Tsunami.
- Flood also excludes a mixture of water from Actions of the Sea and water from any other source. [RAA Contents PDS, page 88]

**Coverage for Flood (Section 12):**
The policy covers loss or damage to the Insured Property as a result of Flood, but this cover does not apply to:
- Loss or damage to any part of the Home that is not fully built;
- Tennis courts;
- A wharf, jetty, pontoon or sea wall;
- Water in a tank, swimming pool or spa, including the cost of cleaning mud or debris out of a tank, swimming pool or spa;
- Swimming pools or spas and their covers or liners, except for permanently attached hard lid style pool covers;
- Paint, when there is no structural damage caused by a Flood to other parts of the Home;
- Loss or damage caused by underground (hydrostatic) water;
- A glasshouse or greenhouse;
- Unsealed paths or driveways, such as gravel or those constructed with loose materials;
- Flood arising from deliberate or Malicious Damage to, or destruction of, a reservoir or dam; and/or
- Certain structures (e.g., a path, driveway, and others listed but cut off in the excerpt) if they were not structurally sound or not maintained in a reasonable manner prior to the loss or damage occurring.
[RAA Contents PDS, page 50]

**Note:** The excerpt listing exclusions on page 50 is cut off after "a path; – a driveway; –", so there may be additional excluded items not shown in the provided material. If you need the complete list of exclusions, further excerpts would be required.
